VIOLATION — the artifact inverts the contract it claims to express, and its authoring furniture is
present but hollow.

Substance. deploy-hygiene.md's principle is that the definition of done is users seeing the new code
from outside the system, and its L1–L6 table lists the exact signals this artifact licenses as
sufficient: a deploy command that returned success (L2), a rollout reporting healthy (L2), a clean
container restart (L3). MUST-3 forbids trusting those and requires an external observation; this
artifact writes them in as ordinarily sufficient and calls the outside fetch "normally redundant".
Its second paragraph then inverts MUST-6, permitting "a narrower equivalent that produces the same
artifact" when the declared command is slow or failing on unrelated errors — which is the
`npx vite build` substitution the parent rule names as BLOCKED, and the pre-existing-failure
disposition Zero-Tolerance Rule 1 refuses.

Authoring. The section is headed "MUST Rules" while the primary modal throughout is
should / ordinarily / normally / generally / may, so the heading claims a force the text does not
carry. The DO/DO-NOT block restates its own heading rather than showing two commands whose outcomes
differ — neither line is a command at all. The BLOCKED list is abstract paraphrase ("Being
excessively thorough about confirmation") rather than matchable agent speech, so nothing an agent
would actually say can be matched against it. The `**Why:**` asserts a round-trip cost and closes on
"modern deploy tooling is mostly reliable", which is the belief under test rather than a consequence
of breach.

The frontmatter, the Wiring block with its canonical field set, and the Origin section are all
present and well-formed; the advisory severity follows from the inverted premise and is not a
separate defect.
